<section
  id="methodology"
  class="methodology"
  aria-labelledby="methodology-heading"
>
  <header>
    <h2 id="methodology-heading">How the model works</h2>
    <p>
      This is a map of illustrative archetypes, not a forecast or a ranking from
      bad to good.
    </p>
  </header>

  <details data-animated-details>
    <summary>
      <span>View methodology and formulas</span>
      <span class="method-chevron" data-disclosure-chevron aria-hidden="true">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 20 20" focusable="false">
          <path d="m5.5 7.5 4.5 4.5 4.5-4.5"></path>
        </svg>
      </span>
    </summary>
    <div class="method-reveal" data-disclosure-reveal>
      <div class="method-reveal-inner" data-disclosure-inner>
        <div class="method-body">
          <section aria-labelledby="coordinates-heading">
            <h3 id="coordinates-heading">Two coordinates</h3>
            <p>
              Shared benefit asks who receives durable claims, shared returns,
              and reliable access. Public agency asks who can contest decisions,
              organize, exercise rights, and exit gatekeepers.
            </p>
            <dl class="formula-list">
              <div>
                <dt>Shared benefit</dt>
                <dd>
                  <code
                    >0.40 &times; ownership + 0.35 &times; shared returns + 0.25
                    &times; universal access</code
                  >
                </dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>Public agency</dt>
                <dd>
                  <code
                    >0.30 &times; democratic accountability + 0.25 &times; civil
                    liberties + 0.25 &times; collective leverage + 0.20 &times;
                    open infrastructure</code
                  >
                </dd>
              </div>
            </dl>
            <p>
              Each institutional slider belongs to one visible axis for
              legibility. This is not a claim that these variables have no
              cross-effects in real societies.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section aria-labelledby="capability-heading">
            <h3 id="capability-heading">Independent capability</h3>
            <p>
              Automation capability is a separate 0-100 dimension. It changes
              the scale and character of consequences, but has no built-in
              social direction.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section aria-labelledby="matching-heading">
            <h3 id="matching-heading">Archetype matching</h3>
            <p>
              For shared benefit B, public agency P, automation A, and an
              archetype center Bi, Pi, Ai:
            </p>
            <code class="equation"
              >distance = sqrt(0.40 &times; (B - Bi)<sup>2</sup> + 0.40 &times; (P
              - Pi)<sup>2</sup> + 0.20 &times; (A - Ai)<sup>2</sup>)</code
            >
            <p>
              The nearest and runner-up are both shown. A gap below 3 is
              “between,” below 8 is “leaning,” and otherwise the first is
              “closest.” A distance of 10 or less is a strong fit, 20 or less is
              moderate, and anything farther is loose. These distances are
              similarities, not probabilities or confidence scores.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section
            class="indicator-formulas"
            aria-labelledby="indicator-heading"
          >
            <h3 id="indicator-heading">Structural indicators</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <code
                  >Material abundance = 0.65 &times; automation + 0.35 &times;
                  universal access</code
                >
              </li>
              <li><code>Shared prosperity = shared benefit</code></li>
              <li>
                <code
                  >Personal autonomy = 0.35 &times; civil liberties + 0.25
                  &times; open infrastructure + 0.25 &times; universal access +
                  0.15 &times; collective leverage</code
                >
              </li>
              <li>
                <code
                  >Political security = 0.45 &times; democratic accountability +
                  0.35 &times; civil liberties + 0.20 &times; collective
                  leverage</code
                >
              </li>
              <li>
                <code
                  >Freedom from compulsory work = automation &times; [0.25 +
                  0.75 &times; (shared benefit / 100)]</code
                >
              </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              Every indicator is clamped to 0-100. These are conceptual
              composites, not measurements.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section aria-labelledby="limits-heading">
            <h3 id="limits-heading">Limitations</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>
                Weights, archetype centers, and match thresholds are normative
                design choices.
              </li>
              <li>
                Real societies contain feedback loops, path dependence, and
                cross-effects.
              </li>
              <li>
                The model omits culture, ecology, conflict, and international
                variation.
              </li>
              <li>Formal institutions may work differently in practice.</li>
              <li>
                Real societies may occupy unmapped combinations or move between
                them over time.
              </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              The purpose is to make assumptions visible and discussable.
              Visitors should challenge the categories, weights, and omissions.
            </p>
          </section>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </details>
</section>

